<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<!--
Fast DDS transport profile: force plain UDPv4 and disable the builtin transports.

Why: by default Fast DDS also brings up shared memory, and on a laptop that is talking
to a vehicle over Ethernet the shared-memory transport buys nothing while adding a
failure mode (stale /dev/shm segments left behind by a container that was killed, which
make discovery hang until they are cleared). Pinning the participant to a single UDPv4
transport keeps the middleware behaviour the same inside and outside the container.

Adapted from the NVIDIA Isaac ROS profile of the same name.
Copyright (c) 2022, NVIDIA CORPORATION. Licensed under the NVIDIA Isaac ROS Software
License.

NOTE: the original file placed a <license> element next to <profiles>, giving it two
root elements. That is not well-formed XML, so Fast DDS rejects the whole file and
silently falls back to its defaults - the profile appears to be loaded but does nothing.
The licence text now lives in this comment, which is where it belongs.

Receive buffer (added 2026-09-15): a raw 1080p bgr8 frame is 6.2 MB and goes out as a
burst of about 100 RTPS fragments. With no size set, each socket gets the kernel default
of about 208 KB, the receiving socket overflows, and because the camera topic is
BEST_EFFORT a frame with a single lost fragment is dropped whole. Measured with a
separate subscriber (ros2 bag record): 5 of 184 frames arrived; with a 32 MB receive
buffer, 186 of 186. The send side never overflowed, so only the receive size is set.

The kernel silently caps this request at net.core.rmem_max. Every topside laptop must
raise that limit once, or this setting does nothing. See TOPSIDE_SETUP.md, section 2.
-->

<profiles xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com/XMLSchemas/fastRTPS_Profiles">
    <transport_descriptors>
        <transport_descriptor>
            <transport_id>CustomUdpTransport</transport_id>
            <type>UDPv4</type>
            <receiveBufferSize>33554432</receiveBufferSize>
        </transport_descriptor>
    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="participant_profile" is_default_profile="true">
        <rtps>
            <userTransports>
                <transport_id>CustomUdpTransport</transport_id>
            </userTransports>

            <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
        </rtps>
    </participant>
</profiles>
